{
  "content": "# B-137a implementation plan\n\n## Public seams and invariant order\n\nThe behavior-facing seams are:\n1. `host_run` submit/foreground attach;\n2. `host_run_status(runId, waitMs<=30000)`;\n3. `host_run_cancel(runId, reason?)`;\n4. `cross_host_review` as the privileged verdict-profile owner;\n5. `review_pass_set` / `finding_add` plus the REVIEW gate as managed-evidence consumers.\n\nPrivate worker, file layout, launch adapters and reconciliation helpers are tested only through those seams or through real child-process effects. Each vertical slice follows RED → minimal GREEN; implementation does not bulk-create imagined internal tests.\n\n## CHK-001 — Persist and reconcile managed host-run lifecycle\n\nCreate a deep `core/managed-host-run.ts` module whose small interface owns submit, attach/status projection, cancel, worker execution and reconcile. A run gets a generated `hrun_*` identity and an immutable root under `.lattice/runtime/host-runs/<runId>/`.\n\nSubmit writes immutable/spec-bound state atomically: `spec.json`, digest-only `owner.json`, 0600 one-shot `input.cap` and `input.prompt`, initial `snapshot.json`, and append-only `events.jsonl`. The hidden worker wins `claim.json` using exclusive create, validates the one-shot capability, unlinks both input files, marks `claimReady`, acquires provider admission, launches the private provider adapter and periodically advances `workerHeartbeatAt`. Provider output updates a separate `lastProviderProgressAt` only when observable; unsupported observability is represented as `unobservable`, never fabricated.\n\nA terminal is created exclusively and exactly once. It records outcome, timing, exit/timeout/cancel reason, provider identity, spec/profile binding digests, evidence journal digest, process cleanup facts, residual process state and an independent `replacementSafe`. Repeated status/cancel/attach calls first acquire a short `reconcile.lock`, re-read facts, and materialize terminal truth for deadline expiry or authoritative worker/provider death. Silence is never a transition predicate. Ambiguous PID/PGID identity yields honest residual `unknown` and `replacementSafe:false`, not blind termination.\n\nInitial RED tests cover: exclusive claim winner, unchanged running state across silent intervals, worker crash followed by deadline reconciliation, one terminal under concurrent reconcilers, idempotent status/cancel, terminal/result digest stability, capability/prompt deletion, and no automatic retry.\n\n## CHK-002 — Expose submit, attach, status and cancel command surfaces\n\nExtend the host-run command schema:\n- `host_run --detach` submits, waits only for a bounded claim handshake, and returns `runId/state/deadlineAt/generation`;\n- foreground `host_run` calls the same submit path then attaches to that run, projecting live output and final usage without owning the provider child;\n- `host_run_status --runId ... --waitMs ...` performs bounded long-poll, returning immediately on terminal or snapshot-generation change and otherwise after at most 30000ms;\n- `host_run_cancel --runId ... --reason ...` writes an idempotent cooperative request and reconciles;\n- hidden `host_run_worker --runId ...` is runtime-only and never advertised as an operator workflow.\n\nDeclare command policy, process-fact level and CLI watchdog behavior for runtime continuation. A foreground caller/watchdog exit may end attachment but cannot convert the managed run to failed or cancel the worker. JSON stdout remains one machine object; text mode keeps live stream routing and concise status guidance. The handler never stores raw prompt/capability in argv or durable public artifacts.\n\nRED starts at CLI/handler seams: detach returns after claim, status wait is capped, foreground and detached share one run/receipt shape, cancel is idempotent, unknown run is fail-closed, and caller termination leaves the run queryable.\n\n## CHK-003 — Supervise provider processes portably and honestly\n\nProvider adapters remain private to ManagedHostRun and preserve canonical launcher specifications. Codex uses documented `codex exec ... -`, the worker writes the prompt to stdin and closes it. Claude retains its canonical launcher contract. Agy is unavailable for ManagedHostRun because its current print launcher accepts the prompt only in argv, so managed execution refuses it before provider spawn until a stdin or protected prompt-file transport exists.\n\nOn POSIX, spawn the provider as a new process group and persist PID/PGID plus a launch fingerprint. Cancellation/deadline cleanup is TERM → bounded grace → KILL → reap → residual probe. Identity is revalidated before signals. On unsupported platforms, managed execution refuses before provider spawn with a structured unsupported-platform terminal; it does not silently degrade to child-only cleanup.\n\nMove the first-output clock to diagnostics only. Preserve finite total timeout/deadline, bounded output projection, provider usage candidate extraction, turn-failure diagnostic, passthrough-degradation honesty and failure classification. Worker heartbeat and provider progress are distinct fields.\n\nRED uses real local Node subprocesses for silent completion, descendant cleanup and TERM-resistant escalation, plus injected provider adapters for Codex argv/stdin closure, overflow and parsing.\n\n## CHK-004 — Enforce shared provider admission independent of usage-ledger mode\n\nExtract the slot registry from the ledger-gated branch into one provider-admission module. Acquisition happens before every provider spawn regardless of `LATTICE_USAGE_LEDGER`; usage preflight/budget/final rows remain conditional and keep existing fail-closed/append-honesty semantics. Slot ownership token, dead-PID reclaim, corrupt-meta stale backstop and token-guarded release remain unchanged.\n\nInventory production provider launch sites. Managed host_run and cross_host_review use ManagedHostRun admission. Direct Codex/Claude/Gemini leaves that are not managed execution are classified by the provider execution registry as `admission_only` or `adapter_only` according to their admission owner; `legacy_unmanaged` is reserved and empty by contract, and `admission_managed` is not a status. Add a source registry/contract smoke so a new direct spawn path fails review until classified.\n\nPreserve candidate-spec per-chunk streaming, 64KiB discard-all overflow and null first-output diagnostics. RED covers ledger-off cap contention, dead/corrupt/live slot reclaim, release race, and source inventory completeness.\n\n## CHK-005 — Route cross-host review through managed execution\n\nKeep `cross_host_review` as profile owner. It still resolves the authoritative story worktree, captures clean HEAD/material/storyFiles facts, authorizes read-only Codex dispatch, creates the detached review snapshot, redacts environment, computes the existing policy timeout ceiling, constructs the structured-verdict prompt and validates the returned verdict schema.\n\nReplace only its direct provider execution with internal ManagedHostRun submit+attach using profile `cross-host-verdict` and opaque bindings. The terminal receipt is included in paired host-verdict provenance. Provider nonzero/timeout/cancel/admission refusal/invalid verdict continue to yield the existing honest blocked/error decision; no raw provider stdout becomes gate evidence.\n\nProtect configured `provider.codex`, `cross_host_review`, bounded retry and fail-closed policies with exact source/runtime regression assertions. RED captures the current direct leaf, then proves the managed path while all existing cross-host checks remain green.\n\n## CHK-006 — Bind formal reviewer evidence to exact managed receipts\n\nAdd a versioned managed-execution binding and a two-phase provider-authored evidence protocol. The profile owner seals opaque bindings at submit: storyId, reviewRunId, lane, intended provider/transport, prompt-envelope/body/context/assembled digests, materialHash, headHash and storyFilesHash. While running, the provider reviewer writes exactly one immutable proposal into the evidence journal: pass or findings plus truthful readManifest/readLog and its registered reviewer identity. It does not call final `review_pass_set` or `finding_add`.\n\nSuccessful terminal creation seals the spec digest, proposal digest and append-only journal digest. After terminal, `managed_review_evidence_import` accepts only a runId/receipt reference, reads the sealed provider-authored bytes, and imports the existing ReviewPass/Finding shape while preserving agent/lane/reviewRun/read provenance exactly. It exposes no verdict/note/finding override fields, is idempotent, and refuses non-success terminal, residual/replacement ambiguity, missing/multiple proposals or any digest mismatch. The controller may trigger import but cannot become the reviewer or reinterpret its verdict.\n\nRegister this managed receipt/composite evidence as a canonical artifact kind in shared Proof Admissibility with producer boundary, scope, REVIEW consumer, claim kind, temporal/material role, semantic field binding and canonical reason codes. Both import-time admission and the REVIEW gate must call the same shared registry/intake/evaluator; process-fact registry declares the gate semantics but is not a substitute. The gate revalidates current terminal, proposal/journal integrity, exact ReviewRun/lane/provider/prompt envelope and current material tuple instead of trusting persisted booleans.\n\nNative-host reviewer lanes retain their existing signed ReviewRun/pass/finding path and are not falsely described as mechanically supervised by ManagedHostRun. Main/implementer/controller-authored evidence, raw CLI output, usage-ledger rows, B-1be5-style time-window matching, pre-terminal proposals and structured degraded decisions cannot upgrade to normal managed receipt assurance.\n\nRED tests the valid provider proposal → successful terminal seal → importer → REVIEW gate path, plus forged runId/terminal/journal/proposal, stale/cross-story/cross-lane/cross-provider/material mismatch, raw CLI absence, controller override/self-assertion, multiple proposals, importer replay, and non-success terminal. A mutation/source-contract test proves both write-time and gate call the shared evaluator: bypassing either call or adding a parallel special-case evaluator must fail.\n\n## CHK-007 — Add adversarial coverage, observability, migration docs and rollout controls\n\nCreate an auto-discovered aggregate smoke so no package.json script is needed. It runs focused managed lifecycle, command, real process-group, admission, cross-host and receipt-gate tests. Add bounded mutation bites that prove premature silence failure, terminal-only monitoring and forged receipt changes are caught. Avoid destructive shared-dist mutations during parallel review.\n\nRegister host-run runtime artifacts with gitignore/material-hash/repair semantics. `doctor`/status diagnostics distinguish worker liveness, provider progress observability, deadline, cancel state, residuals, replacement safety and retention. GC removes only terminal runs beyond retention; active runs and unconsumed one-shot inputs are protected, while failed startup cleanup removes secrets.\n\nUpdate `docs/host-run-usage.md` and add `docs/requirements/decisions/managed-cross-provider-cli-execution.md` as the durable decision truth. Document exact operator commands, 30-second wait semantics, deadline reconciliation, no silence-based restart, the `managed`/`admission_only`/`adapter_only`/`readiness_probe` registry terms, that `legacy_unmanaged` is reserved and `admission_managed` is not a status, the local same-uid trust ceiling, and that native subagents are out of scope.\n\nRollout is compatibility-first for non-review agent runs but enforcement is immediate for the new managed CLI-review evidence class: a raw CLI execution may still be used manually, but it cannot satisfy a formal managed reviewer gate. Final verification includes build, focused aggregate smoke, existing host-run/cross-host/provider/dispatch/review/gate suites, process/runtime registry contracts, source scans, protected timeout diff, and a real caller-detach scenario whose original run reaches terminal without a duplicate.\n"
}
